---
import Guide from "../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import DocLink from "../../components/DocLink.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Customizing</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    Theme tokens change the whole library. To change one component, or one
    instance of it, set its <code>--_*</code> properties.
  </Fragment>

  <section>
    <h2 id="your-theme">Your theme</h2>
    <p>
      A theme is the <DocLink href="/guide/concepts#token-tiers"
        >knobs, roles and component tokens</DocLink
      > you change, in the <code>theme.custom</code> layer, right after the defaults.
      Tokens you leave out keep their defaults, also new ones in later releases.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`@layer theme.custom {
  :where(html) {
    --ui-radius: 4px;
    --ui-density: 0.9;
    --ui-font-body: "Inter", var(--font-sans);
    --ui-font-heading: "Fraunces", var(--font-serif);
    --ui-palette-chroma: 0.35;
    --ui-palette-hue: 265;
  }
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <p>
      If it might load before <code>layers.css</code>, start it with the two <code
        >@layer</code
      > statements from there, like the example themes do. The <DocLink
        href="/guide/theme-generator">Theme generator</DocLink
      > writes one for you.
    </p>
    <h3 id="example-themes">Example themes</h3>
    <p>
      Four themes ship with the package to show how far tokens go. Each is only
      tokens in <code>theme.custom</code>, no component CSS, and every stress
      test page runs with each of them in light and dark mode. They copy their
      originals faithfully, also where those fall short of WCAG contrast (the
      translucent focus rings of Bootstrap and shadcn, the bright blue of
      brutalist). They name their fonts (Geist, Roboto, DM Sans) but don't load
      them. Try them in the theme drawer in the header.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>bootstrap</code>: Bootstrap 5. Its <code>#0d6efd</code> blue, grays,
        38px controls, regular weight buttons and labels, heading scale and the wide
        translucent focus ring.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>brutalist</code>: neobrutalism. Black 2px borders, hard 4px
        shadows that buttons press into on hover, a bright blue on pale blue and
        heavy type.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>m3</code>: Material 3. The baseline purple scheme and its tinted
        surfaces, pill buttons in the primary purple that lift on hover,
        outlined ones in the outline gray, 12px cards, 4px outlined fields,
        Roboto, the type scale and the 3px focus ring.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>shadcn</code>: shadcn/ui. Neutral grays, a near-black primary,
        36px controls with 14px text, light borders with a faint shadow, a 3px
        gray focus ring and rounder cards than controls.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      code={`@import "opui-css/css/imports.css";
@import "opui-css/themes/brutalist.css";`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="component-properties">Component properties</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>--_*</code> private properties are each component's wiring, the last
      <DocLink href="/guide/concepts#token-tiers">tier</DocLink> before its CSS. Every
      component page lists its own under Component properties, marked as inputs or
      outputs, and components share names where they mean the same thing. Button reads
      these:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>--_accent</code>: the accent color. Fills, borders, text, hover
        and active states are all derived from it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--_bg-color</code> and <code>--_text-color</code>: the current
        background and text color.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--_duration</code> and <code>--_ease</code>: the transition
        duration and easing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--_icon-size</code>: the size of a direct child <code>svg</code>.
      </li>
      <li><code>--_min-height</code>: the height, set by the size classes.</li>
      <li>
        <code>--_motion</code>: the motion multiplier, <code>0</code> turns transitions
        off.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--_padding-inline</code>: the inline padding, set by the size
        classes.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The component declares them on itself, so set them on the component, not
      on a parent. Theme tokens are the ones to set on a parent:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`/* Does nothing: every .ui-button declares its own --_icon-size */
.toolbar {
  --_icon-size: 1.25em;
}

/* Works */
.toolbar .ui-button {
  --_icon-size: 1.25em;
}

/* Theme tokens inherit, so a parent is fine */
.toolbar {
  --ui-button-radius: var(--radius-round);
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <Example centered>
      <button
        class="ui-button ui-filled"
        style="--_accent: var(--ui-green)"
        type="button"
      >
        Green filled
      </button>
      <button
        class="ui-button ui-outlined"
        style="--_accent: var(--ui-green)"
        type="button"
      >
        Green outlined
      </button>
    </Example>
    <p>
      Prefer inputs like <code>--_accent</code> over outputs like <code
        >--_bg-color</code
      >. The states set the outputs, so a new <code>--_bg-color</code> freezes the
      background in every state, while a new <code>--_accent</code> flows through
      all of them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="unlayered-or-your-own-layer">Unlayered or your own layer</h2>
    <p>
      Unlayered CSS beats every <DocLink href="/guide/concepts#cascade-layers"
        >layer</DocLink
      >, which is fine for most apps. If you use layers yourself, add one after <code
        >utils</code
      >: it beats the library and still loses to your unlayered one-off fixes.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`@layer openprops, theme, normalize, components.prose, components.root, components.extended, utils, overrides;
@layer theme.palette, theme.base, theme.custom;

@layer overrides {
  .ui-badge {
    --_size: var(--size-5);
  }
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>
</Guide>
